<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adamawa Emirate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a>–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Fombina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Adamawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> state founded around 1809 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modibbo-adama">Modibbo Adama</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>, as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. Centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yola">Yola</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benue-river">Benue River</a>, it expanded over the highlands of what are now north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>. In 1901 it was divided between British Nigeria and German Kamerun, and the Lamido of Adamawa still reigns at Yola.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adamawa-emirate.jpg" alt="Adamawa Emirate" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 7bdIa9 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adamawa_State_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Fulbe first appear near the region as envoys of the emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu</a> in the 13th century, and more followed into Hausaland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>, valued for their Islamic learning. Mai Dunama ibn Hajj Ali of Bornu exempted a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> ardo and his people from taxes, but at the turn of the 18th century Mai Hamdan ibn Dunama broke the agreement, and harassment, famine and Bornu's decline drove many Fulbe on to the Adamawa region. Adama, born around 1770, received a flag from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> in March 1809 to lead the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> in Fombina, a name meaning the southlands. The name Adamawa, people of Adama, came later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Adamawa held over 42 sub-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a>, or lamidates, some fairly autonomous, such as Tibati, founded by Hamman Sambo in 1818, and Ngaoundéré. Their lamidos were elected by local councils subject to approval from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yola">Yola</a>, which often had to settle their quarrels. Like other emirates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, Adamawa was largely self-governing but paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a>. Lamido Lauwal formalised the ruler's council with the titles of Galadima, Waziri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alkali">Alkali</a>, Agia and Sarkin Yaki, none of them hereditary. There was no standing army: volunteers fought. By some estimates slaves, called jeyaɓe, were about half the population by the late 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Adama moved his capital from Gurin to Ribadu in 1831 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yola">Yola</a> in 1841, and died in February 1847. Lauwal ruled until 1872 and Sanda until 1890. Sanda let the National African Company, later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-niger-company">Royal Niger Company</a>, trade at Yola in 1883, but refused to receive the British commissioner Claude MacDonald in 1889. Under Zubairu bin Adama the Germans invaded southern Adamawa in January 1898 and took Tibati.</p><p>Debating whether to resist the British, the court divided; Zubairu led the party of resistance, against the Qadi Hamman Joda and members of his own family. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yola">Yola</a> fell to the British he wrote to Sultan Abdurrahman that there would be no surrender to the unbeliever even after the fall of the strongholds.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adamawa Emirate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Muhammad Bello and brother Abdullahi dan Fodio led the campaigns against Gobir, while flagbearers such as Sulaimanu in Kano, Musa in Zaria and Modibbo Adama in <b>Adamawa</b> carved out new emirates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It consisted of two separate areas: the Northern Cameroons, bordering Bornu and <b>Adamawa</b>, and the Southern Cameroons, with Buea as its centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-cameroons">British Cameroons</a></li></ul></section>
